%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison---008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON)---differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 0.97\times$ (eval 503.0). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 53 of 56 experimental configurations across two environments ($n{=}56$; AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\pm$ seed range). Trained $\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seeds~4 and~5 data in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding precedes CIVO in all five Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE $1.53$--$4.72\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citealt{agarwal2021precipice}). Including replications and Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset is universal across all ten tested runs ($n{=}10$, two multi-joint locomotion environments). Sustained collapse occurs in 6/10 runs; three runs self-correct (Walker seed~2 and both Cheetah seeds) and one shows onset without sustained collapse (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Mechanism evidence.}
The RSSM reward head develops a $3.11\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (reward MAE $0.057 \to 0.178$; independently corroborated at $2.78\times$ from earlier checkpoints; Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), while observation reconstruction \emph{improves} with temporal conditioning (gap $1.33\times$ vs.\ baseline $1.46\times$), confirming a reward-specific pathology. Direct value calibration confirms the critic internalizes this bias: the CIVO model's mean $V(s)/\text{MC-return}$ ratio reaches $7.80\times$ (median $3.40\times$; 100\% of sampled states overestimated) vs.\ median $1.05\times$ for the nearly-calibrated control (52.5\% overestimated). Causal ordering further supports a value-channel pathology: evaluation return collapses ${\sim}35$K steps before actor entropy rises ($+2.1$ nats/10K in collapsed configurations vs.\ $+0.07$--$0.22$ in healthy baselines; Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Temporal ordering ($n{=}2$).}
Time-series analysis across 12 dense checkpoints (10K--65K steps) provides evidence that reward fidelity degradation precedes value overestimation onset: the single-step fidelity ratio ($\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}2}/\text{MAE}_{\dt{=}1}$) crosses $1.1\times$ at 20K steps, while the value-to-eval ratio begins rising at ${\sim}$30K (reaching $1.59\times$ at 40K)---a ${\sim}$10K onset-to-onset lag ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$; Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_ordering}). A second seed (exp\_066b) confirms the pattern: fidelity gap onset at ${\sim}$15K precedes VE onset at ${\sim}$24K (temporal lead ${\approx}$9K steps, consistent with seed~0's ${\approx}$10K), though seed~1's peak VE ($0.72\times$) remained below the $1.5\times$ threshold within 60K steps, suggesting seed-dependent progression rates. The gap arises from differential improvement: $\dt{=}1$ MAE decreases continuously ($0.093 \to 0.047$) while $\dt{=}2$ MAE plateaus (${\sim}0.08$; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone precedes pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 3 seeds, peak NVE $\leq 1.41\times$) both remain below $1.5\times$. Cross-environment replication on Cheetah Run confirms generalization. Freezing the embedding (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}) dramatically reduces severity (NVE $0.19$--$1.88\times$ vs.\ trained $1.53$--$4.72\times$; 2/4 seeds below threshold, 2/4 transient self-correcting spikes; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}); removing the embedding (008f) also avoids CIVO. A controlled three-way ablation varying \emph{only} embedding treatment (trained/frozen/removed, single seed; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}) confirms that gradient training---not embedding presence---is necessary. Extending to additional seeds and environments, the onset rate remains 10/10 multi-joint locomotion runs (Fisher exact $p < 0.0001$ vs.\ 0/8 controls). Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (peak NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction (final eval 538, 59\% baseline; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}), confirming that \emph{any} gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ suffices to induce CIVO. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO. All value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific: frozen controls show comparable or higher peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), and all self-correct. CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion in DreamerV3; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination eliminates CIVO.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Across four unique seeds (s0, s1, s2, s5), none exhibits CIVO onset (peak NVE ${\leq}0.6\times$, well below the $1.5\times$ threshold). Three of four seeds achieve $64$--$94\%$ of baseline (eval $597$--$877$; mean $775.6$); seed~0 exhibits CIVO-unrelated learning stagnation (eval $148$, $16\%$ baseline; Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). This confirms that multi-step imagination---not the trained embedding alone---is necessary for CIVO induction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s2 ($\dt{=}1$ imagination), no CIVO onset (NVE $0.5\times$, eval $64\%$ baseline).}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Full-SGS (exp\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, NVE $1.66\times$); seed~2 collapses (0/2 elimination; Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah (010b, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$) confirms CIVO generalization; full-SGS yields 0/3 elimination. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO on Cheetah (024b, NVE $< 0.2\times$).

\paragraph{Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS confirms pathway redundancy.}
Obs-decoder-only SGS (exp\_025/026) achieves 1/6 CIVO prevention (1/4 Walker, 0/2 Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}), not meaningfully different from full-pathway SGS (0/3). CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels: blocking any single pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Intervention hierarchy. Prevention = peak NVE $< 1.5\times$ (35K+). No level reliably eliminates CIVO across environments.}
\label{tab:intervention_hierarchy}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Level} & \textbf{Intervention} & \textbf{Seeds} & \textbf{Prev.} & \textbf{Rate} \\
\midrule
--- & Untreated & 10 & 0/10 & 0\% \\
\midrule
Gradient & Reward-only SGS & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Gradient & Full-pathway SGS & 3 & 0/3 & 0\% \\
Gradient & Obs-decoder SGS & 6 & 1/6 & 17\% \\
\midrule
Output & RAVE ($c{=}1.5{,}2.0$) & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
\midrule
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.1$) & 1 & 1/1$^\ddagger$ & 100\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.2$) & 6$^\dagger$ & 2/6 & 33\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}0.5$) & 3 & 0/3 & 0\% \\
Repr. & TCN ($\sigma{=}1.0$) & 1 & 1/1$^\S$ & 100\% \\
\midrule
Arch. & Wide single & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Arch. & Narrow dual (min) & 2 & 0/2 & 0\% \\
Arch. & Wide dual (min) & 4 & 3/4$^\|$ & 75\% \\
\midrule
--- & Embedding frozen & 7 & 5/7$^\#$ & 71\% \\
--- & Embedding removed & 5 & 5/5 & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Walker 2/4 (s2 NVE $2.11\times$; s3 late-onset CIVO, NVE $3.04\times$ at 105K); Cheetah 0/1, PickCube 0/1.
$^\ddagger$Borderline (NVE${=}1.02\times$, eval 55\%).
$^\S$CIVO prevented but progressive policy erosion (peak 116\%, final 15\% baseline).
$^\|$Walker 3/3 ($\nve \leq 1.4\times$; 1/3 non-CIVO eval collapse), Cheetah 0/1.\\
$^\#$Walker 5 + Cheetah 2. Exp\_047 (NVE $1.71\times$) and Exp\_057 (NVE $1.88\times$): both transient, self-correcting within 20K steps; remaining 5 seeds: NVE $0.19$--$1.45\times$.\par}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% Forward-pass interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_results}

We test two forward-pass alternatives (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). \textbf{RAVE} (value-level capping) fails: the EMA anchor tracks biased values during CIVO onset. \textbf{TCN} (representation-level noise) suppresses CIVO in 2 of 4 Walker seeds at $\sigma{=}0.2$ (NVE $< 1.0\times$, eval 45--91\%), but remaining seeds show runaway escalation, suggesting TCN gates rather than attenuates CIVO. Cross-environment transfer fails: Cheetah effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$) causes policy erosion to 15\% baseline; PickCube 0/1 (Appendix~\ref{sec:tcn_cross_env}). TCN is a \emph{diagnostic probe}, not a deployable mitigation.

%% ============================================================
%% Architecture-Level Ablation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Architecture-Level Ablation}
\label{sec:arch_results}

\paragraph{Critic convergence in pure imagination.}
Across all dual-critic configurations ($n{=}7$), critics trained on shared RSSM features converge ($\rho > 0.999$), with pessimism at 3--4\% of the independent-critic bound (\S\ref{sec:dual_critic_method}). Pessimistic estimation is thus ineffective; the min operation in wide dual-critic may provide regularization through mechanisms that remain to be characterized (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

\paragraph{$2{\times}2$ factorial: recovery vs.\ prevention.}
Min drives recovery: narrow dual-critic (${\sim}1.2$M) recovers fully (${\sim}16$--$19\times$ recovery ratio) despite $2\times$ fewer parameters. Prevention requires min $+$ capacity: wide dual-critic maintains $\nve \leq 1.4\times$ (3/3 prevention, 1/3 non-CIVO collapse). Cross-environment transfer is limited (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

%% ============================================================
%% Online CIVO Detection
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

We derive a lightweight online detector monitoring the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth (Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}). Applied to 14 runs across three environments, it achieves TPR${=}5/5$ (95\% Clopper-Pearson CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$), FPR${=}2/9$ ($22\%$), with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases (Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). The detector requires larger-sample validation before deployment as a practical diagnostic tool.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Online CIVO Detector. Monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio $r_t = \bar{V}_t / \bar{E}_t$ (not NVE) for superlinear growth indicative of CIVO onset.}
\label{alg:civo_detector}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Stream of $(\bar{V}_t, \bar{E}_t)$ at each checkpoint $t$
\STATE $\tau_\text{mag} \leftarrow 0.49$, \quad $\tau_\text{vel} \leftarrow 0.1$, \quad $\varepsilon \leftarrow 10^{-6}$
\STATE Initialize $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\mathcal{H} \leftarrow []$
\FOR{each checkpoint $t$}
  \STATE $r_t \leftarrow \bar{V}_t \,/\, \max(\bar{E}_t,\, \varepsilon)$
  \STATE $\Delta r_t \leftarrow r_t - r_\text{prev}$
  \STATE $c_\text{mag} \leftarrow (r_t \geq \tau_\text{mag})$
  \STATE $c_\text{vel} \leftarrow (\Delta r_t > \tau_\text{vel})$
  \STATE $c_\text{acc} \leftarrow (\Delta r_\text{prev} > \tau_\text{vel}) \wedge (\Delta r_t > \Delta r_\text{prev})$
  \STATE $c_\text{dec} \leftarrow \bigl|\{i : \mathcal{H}[i] < \mathcal{H}[i{-}1],\; i \in \text{last 3}\}\bigr| \geq 2$
  \IF{$c_\text{mag} \wedge c_\text{vel} \wedge c_\text{acc} \wedge c_\text{dec}$}
    \STATE \textbf{Alert:} CIVO onset at step $t$
  \ENDIF
  \STATE $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow r_t$, \quad $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow \Delta r_t$
  \STATE Append $\bar{E}_t$ to $\mathcal{H}$
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

